{"description":"`toUnit` in `exchange/builder.go` never set `Unit.Provenance`, so exporting a repository dropped provenance entirely: an `inferred` or `reconciled` declaration left the building as the human default, and export -\u003e import -\u003e export could not reproduce what it started from. `Unit.ToArtifact` already read the field; nothing on the way out wrote it, and `buildFrontmatter`/`buildJSONAuthoring` had no provenance key to render.","impact":"Fixed by projecting provenance in `toUnit` and rendering it from one decision point (`artifactProvenance`) shared by both renderers. Owner decision for ADR-035: the exchange unit is authoritative for provenance during transport, and absence means human — the default is normalised to empty on the way out because the Markdown and JSON adapters report the same undeclared claim differently, so spelling `human` would make a Markdown export differ in bytes from the JSON re-import of itself. `TestImportRoundTrip` needed no weakening: no assertion changed, the normalisation sits at the read boundary. A value outside the enum is refused before any byte is written, since the Markdown adapter reads no provenance and an invalid value there would vanish with no finding.","acceptanceCriteria":"- Exporting a repository whose artifact declares `provenance: inferred` or `reconciled` produces a unit carrying that value, and export -\u003e import -\u003e export is byte-identical.\n- An artifact with no provenance declaration gains none through the cycle.\n- A unit declaring a value outside human|inferred|reconciled is refused at import, before any write."}